{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778080060,"nonce":"1jZNW1gEi6zAXc9xRhj4ZuovH74=","title":"AbstractSkeleton/Mechanics: `linearize` protocol for downstream analysis tools","message":"Several downstream feedbax users need to linearize the plant dynamics around a nominal operating point — for H∞ Riccati control synthesis, induced-gain `||T_{w→z}||_∞` estimation, Jacobian/Hessian-based diagnostics, fixed-point analysis, etc.\n\nCurrently each tool reconstructs the linearized state-space matrices for the augmented state `[pos, vel, F]` from `PointMass.A`, `PointMass.B` and the documented force-filter dynamics by hand. As more analysis tools land, this duplication compounds and risks drift between tools.\n\n## Proposed\n\nAdd a `linearize` protocol on `AbstractSkeleton` (or `Mechanics`) that returns continuous-time `(A, B, B_w)` matrices around a nominal state.\n\nConcrete API outline:\n\n```python\nclass AbstractSkeleton(eqx.Module):\n    def linearize(self, state: State) -\u003e LinearSystem:\n        '''Continuous-time A, B, B_w matrices around `state`.'''\n        ...\n```\n\n`LinearSystem` would be a small `eqx.Module` containing `A_c, B_c, Bw_c` and slot indices for pos / vel / force decomposition. A `Mechanics`-level wrapper would compose the skeleton linearization with the force-filter LPF to produce the augmented-state linearization.\n\n## Motivation\n\n- rlrmp `src/rlrmp/analysis/hinf_riccati.py` (just landed; rlrmp PR-equivalent merged via auth request `8387dfa4`) implements this directly for `PointMass`. The skeleton itself knows its own state structure, so the math belongs in feedbax not rlrmp.\n- Future `LinearDynamicsAdversary` (per rlrmp synthesis_review §11 step 4) needs `ΔA` in the same coordinate system; reusing one canonical linearization avoids drift.\n- Induced-gain analyser, cross-partial `∇²_{θδ}L` diagnostic — same pattern.\n- Generalises cleanly to the 2-link arm skeleton when that lands (currently handled ad-hoc in JAX).\n\n## Scope\n\nLift the rlrmp pointmass linearization into feedbax as a method on `PointMass` and a documented protocol on `AbstractSkeleton`. ~30-50 LOC plus tests. Lightweight first pass; the broader `Mechanics.linearize_closed_loop` for full neural policies + plant is a separate issue if needed.\n\n## References\n\n- `/Users/mll/Main/10 Projects/10 PhD/rlrmp/src/rlrmp/analysis/hinf_riccati.py` — concrete implementation pattern.\n- `/Users/mll/Main/10 Projects/10 PhD/rlrmp/results/part2_5/synthesis_review.md` §11 — context for the downstream uses.","files":null}]}